M="pharm-resp"
BOSS=dict(th="เคสใหญ่: หอบกลางดึก",en="Boss case: Wheezing at midnight",
case="A 19-year-old woman with asthma uses her albuterol inhaler 4–5 days a week and wakes at night with cough and wheeze twice a week. She arrives in the ED after a cold with severe dyspnea, speaking in short phrases; SpO₂ 90%, diffuse wheeze.",
qs=[
("First-line bronchodilator in the ED:",["Nebulized albuterol (SABA)","Oral theophylline loading dose","Inhaled salmeterol (LABA) alone","Oral montelukast tablet"],0,"Repeated SABA (often with ipratropium) is the first step in an acute exacerbation."),
("Which systemic therapy reduces relapse after this exacerbation?",["Systemic corticosteroids","Empiric IV antibiotics","Oral H₁ antihistamine","Inhaled cromolyn sodium"],0,"Oral or IV steroids speed recovery and reduce relapse in moderate–severe attacks."),
("Albuterol acts by:",["M₃ blockade → ↓IP₃ → less airway muscle contraction","β₂ agonism → ↑cAMP → airway smooth muscle relaxation","5-lipoxygenase inhibition → ↓leukotriene synthesis","Mast cell stabilization → ↓mediator release"],1,"β₂–Gs–adenylyl cyclase–cAMP–PKA relaxes bronchial smooth muscle."),
("A common adverse effect to monitor with repeated albuterol:",["Hyperkalemia and weakness","Hypokalemia and tremor","Hypoglycemia and sweating","Bradycardia and heart block"],1,"β₂ agonists shift K⁺ into cells and cause tremor and tachycardia."),
("At discharge, the best controller for her uncontrolled persistent asthma:",["Salmeterol (LABA) alone","ICS–formoterol inhaler","Oral theophylline alone","As-needed albuterol only"],1,"Inhaled corticosteroid is the core controller; LABA must never be used without ICS (↑ asthma deaths)."),
("A key local adverse effect of inhaled corticosteroids:",["Oropharyngeal candidiasis","Gingival hyperplasia","Ototoxicity (hearing loss)","Peripheral neuropathy"],0,"Thrush and dysphonia; rinse mouth and use a spacer."),
],pass_=4)
T={
"targets":[("Which drug class targets IgE directly?",["Leukotriene receptor antagonists","Anti-IgE antibody (omalizumab)","Methylxanthines (theophylline)","Mast cell stabilizers (cromolyn)"],1,"Omalizumab is a monoclonal antibody that binds free IgE, preventing binding to FcεRI on mast cells."),
 ("Bronchoconstriction in asthma is mediated mainly by:",["β₂ receptors via ↑cAMP signaling","M₃ receptors, leukotrienes, histamine","α₂ receptors on airway smooth muscle","Nicotinic receptors at the NMJ"],1,"Parasympathetic M₃ tone and mast-cell mediators (LTC₄/D₄, histamine) constrict airways."),
 ('A 55-year-old man with mild asthma is started on propranolol for essential tremor. Two days later he presents with cough, chest tightness and diffuse expiratory wheeze. The mechanism of his bronchospasm is:', ['Blockade of β₂-mediated airway smooth muscle relaxation', 'Direct agonism of M₃ receptors on airway smooth muscle', 'Increased leukotriene synthesis from COX inhibition', 'IgE-mediated mast cell degranulation by the drug'], 0, 'Nonselective β-blockers remove β₂–cAMP bronchodilator tone, leaving M₃ and mediator constriction unopposed; leukotriene shunting is the mechanism of aspirin-exacerbated disease, not β-blockade.', 2)],
"beta2":[("Which β₂ agonist is long-acting?",["Albuterol","Levalbuterol","Salmeterol","Terbutaline (inhaled)"],2,"Salmeterol and formoterol are LABAs; albuterol is a SABA."),
 ("LABA monotherapy in asthma is avoided because:",["It causes marked hyperkalemia","It raises asthma-related deaths","It has no bronchodilator effect","It frequently causes oral thrush"],1,"LABA alone increases risk of severe exacerbation and death; LABAs are always combined with an ICS in asthma."),
 ('A 16-year-old girl with asthma uses albuterol many times over a day during a cold. She notes fine hand tremor and palpitations; HR 118/min, serum K⁺ 3.1 mEq/L, creatinine normal. The low potassium is best explained by:', ['β₂-stimulated Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase shifting K⁺ into cells', 'Increased renal K⁺ loss via ENaC activation', 'Aldosterone release driven by β₁ stimulation', 'Gastrointestinal K⁺ loss from increased motility'], 0, 'β₂ agonism activates Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase and drives K⁺ intracellularly (a transcellular shift); total-body K⁺ and renal handling are unchanged.', 3)],
"steroids":[("Corticosteroids reduce airway inflammation chiefly by:",["Blocking β₂ receptors in airway muscle","Inhibiting NF-κB cytokine transcription","Blocking H₁ receptors on airway muscle","Inhibiting phosphodiesterase → ↑cAMP"],1,"Glucocorticoids suppress inflammatory gene transcription; first-line controller in chronic asthma."),
 ("Which is a systemic effect of long-term oral steroids?",["Osteoporosis and hyperglycemia","Hypoglycemia and weight loss","Hyperkalemia and hypotension","Bradycardia and constipation"],0,"Cushingoid effects: osteoporosis, hyperglycemia, adrenal suppression, infection."),
 ('A 45-year-old woman with severe asthma has needed prednisone 20 mg daily for 8 months. She now has sudden back pain; x-ray shows a T12 compression fracture and DEXA T-score is −2.8. The main mechanism of her bone loss is:', ['Decreased osteoblast function and bone formation', 'Increased PTH from parathyroid hyperplasia', 'Excess vitamin D activation with hypercalciuria', 'Osteoclast inhibition with defective remodeling'], 0, 'Glucocorticoids chiefly suppress osteoblasts (and reduce Ca²⁺ absorption), causing osteoporosis; they do not cause parathyroid hyperplasia.', 3)],
"antimusc":[("Tiotropium is best described as:",["SAMA","LAMA","LABA","PDE4 inhibitor"],1,"Tiotropium is a long-acting M₃ antagonist used in COPD and asthma."),
 ("A typical adverse effect of inhaled antimuscarinics:",["Dry mouth","Diarrhea","Bradycardia","Hypersalivation"],0,"Anticholinergic effects: dry mouth; caution with glaucoma and urinary retention."),
 ('A 72-year-old man with COPD and benign prostatic hyperplasia starts a new once-daily inhaler for persistent dyspnea. One week later he has difficulty voiding and a palpable, distended bladder. Which drug was most likely started?', ['Tiotropium', 'Salmeterol', 'Fluticasone', 'Roflumilast'], 0, 'Tiotropium is an inhaled antimuscarinic (LAMA) that can precipitate urinary retention in BPH; salmeterol causes tremor and tachycardia, not retention.', 2)],
"lt":[("Zileuton inhibits:",["5-lipoxygenase","LTD₄ receptor (CysLT1)","COX-2","Phospholipase A₂"],0,"Zileuton blocks 5-LOX; montelukast/zafirlukast block CysLT1 receptors."),
 ("Montelukast is especially useful in:",["Aspirin-exacerbated respiratory disease","Acute severe asthma (status asthmaticus)","COPD exacerbation with hypercapnia","Pulmonary arterial hypertension"],0,"Aspirin-induced asthma involves shunting to leukotrienes; neuropsychiatric effects are a boxed warning."),
 ('A 38-year-old woman with asthma and recurrent nasal polyps develops wheeze, rhinorrhea and facial flushing within an hour of taking ibuprofen for a headache. An oral add-on controller is chosen for her condition. Its mechanism is:', ['Blocks the CysLT1 leukotriene receptor', 'Inhibits phosphodiesterase-4 in leukocytes', 'Antagonizes H₁ receptors on airway muscle', 'Stabilizes mast cells to block degranulation'], 0, 'Aspirin-exacerbated respiratory disease shunts arachidonic acid to leukotrienes, so montelukast (CysLT1 antagonist) is useful; PDE4 inhibition is roflumilast for COPD.', 3)],
"biologics":[("Mepolizumab and reslizumab target:",["IL-5","IgE","IL-4Rα","TSLP"],0,"Anti-IL-5 for eosinophilic asthma; benralizumab targets the IL-5 receptor."),
 ("Dupilumab blocks:",["IL-5 (eosinophil growth)","IL-4Rα (IL-4/IL-13)","Free IgE (FcεRI binding)","TSLP (epithelial alarmin)"],1,"Used for eosinophilic asthma, atopic dermatitis and nasal polyps."),
 ('A 50-year-old woman has severe asthma with 3 exacerbations in the past year despite high-dose ICS–LABA and good technique. Blood eosinophils are 650/µL and total IgE is normal. Which biologic best targets the main driver of her disease?', ['Mepolizumab', 'Omalizumab', 'Ustekinumab', 'Rituximab'], 0, 'Eosinophilic asthma responds to anti-IL-5 (mepolizumab); omalizumab targets IgE in allergic asthma with raised IgE.', 2)],
"pde":[("Theophylline has a narrow therapeutic index; toxicity causes:",["Seizures and arrhythmias","Bradycardia and hypotension","Constipation and ileus","Hypoglycemia and lethargy"],0,"Methylxanthine; metabolized by CYP1A2, so CYP inhibitors raise levels."),
 ("Roflumilast is used for:",["Severe COPD with chronic bronchitis","Acute severe asthma in the ED","Cystic fibrosis lung disease","Pulmonary arterial hypertension"],0,"PDE4 inhibitor ↑cAMP in inflammatory cells; reduces COPD exacerbations."),
 ('A 66-year-old man with severe COPD and chronic productive cough (FEV₁ 40%) has 2 exacerbations a year despite LAMA + LABA. An oral drug is added that raises cAMP in inflammatory cells and reduces exacerbations. Which adverse effects are most expected?', ['Diarrhea, nausea and weight loss', 'Hyperkalemia and bradycardia', 'Oral thrush and hoarseness', 'Urinary retention and dry mouth'], 0, 'Roflumilast (PDE4 inhibitor) commonly causes GI upset and weight loss; thrush and hoarseness come from inhaled corticosteroids.', 3)],
"antihist":[("First-generation H₁ antihistamines differ from second-generation by:",["Lower lipophilicity and less CNS entry","Crossing the blood–brain barrier → sedation","Lacking antiemetic and anticholinergic effects","Longer half-life allowing once-daily dosing"],1,"They also have anticholinergic effects. Diphenhydramine and chlorpheniramine sedate; loratadine and fexofenadine do not."),
 ("Pseudoephedrine relieves nasal congestion through:",["H₁ blockade (↓ histamine effect)","α-adrenergic agonism (vasoconstriction)","M₃ blockade (↓ secretions)","Mast cell stabilization (↓ release)"],1,"Adverse effects: hypertension, CNS stimulation; rebound congestion with topical agents."),
 ("A 78-year-old man takes an over-the-counter sleep aid at the labeled dose. The next morning he is confused, with dry mouth, dilated pupils and urinary retention. The drug's effects are best explained by:", ['Muscarinic receptor antagonism', 'Histamine H₂ receptor antagonism', 'α₁-adrenergic receptor agonism', 'Dopamine D₂ receptor agonism'], 0, 'First-generation H₁ antihistamines such as diphenhydramine are lipophilic and anticholinergic, a Beers-list hazard in the elderly; they have no clinically relevant H₂ effect.', 3)],
"cough":[("Dextromethorphan suppresses cough via:",["Peripheral μ-opioid agonism in airways","NMDA antagonism in the cough center","H₁ blockade in the nasopharynx","Mucolysis by breaking disulfide bonds"],1,"Acts centrally in the medulla; little analgesic or addictive potential; serotonin syndrome with SSRIs."),
 ("N-acetylcysteine as a mucolytic works by:",["Breaking disulfide bonds in mucus","Blocking M₃ receptors on glands","Stimulating ciliary beat frequency","Inhibiting leukotriene synthesis"],0,"Thiol cleaves disulfide bonds; guaifenesin is an expectorant."),
 ('A 34-year-old woman taking sertraline uses an OTC cough syrup at the labeled dose for a cold. Two days later she is agitated and diaphoretic with temperature 38.6 °C, tremor, hyperreflexia and inducible clonus. Which cough-syrup ingredient is most likely responsible?', ['Dextromethorphan', 'Guaifenesin', 'Benzonatate', 'N-acetylcysteine'], 0, 'Dextromethorphan inhibits serotonin reuptake and can cause serotonin syndrome with SSRIs; guaifenesin is an expectorant without serotonergic action.', 3)],
"pah":[("Bosentan acts by:",["Phosphodiesterase-5 inhibition","Endothelin-1 receptor antagonism","Prostacyclin (IP) receptor agonism","Soluble guanylate cyclase stimulation"],1,"Hepatotoxic; monitor LFTs; teratogenic."),
 ("Sildenafil treats PAH by:",["PDE5 inhibition → ↑cGMP","Endothelin receptor blockade","IP receptor activation → ↑cAMP","L-type Ca²⁺ channel blockade"],0,"↑cGMP causes pulmonary vasodilation. Prostacyclin analogs (epoprostenol) and riociguat are other options."),
 ('A 32-year-old woman has exertional dyspnea and a loud P₂. Right heart catheterization shows mean PA pressure 42 mm Hg, wedge pressure 10 mm Hg and a negative vasoreactivity test. She starts an oral drug that requires monthly liver tests and pregnancy tests. Its mechanism is:', ['Endothelin-1 receptor antagonism', 'Phosphodiesterase-5 inhibition', 'Prostacyclin receptor agonism', 'L-type Ca²⁺ channel blockade'], 0, 'Bosentan (endothelin receptor antagonist) is hepatotoxic and teratogenic; PDE5 inhibitors such as sildenafil need no routine LFT or pregnancy monitoring.', 3)],
"delivery":[("A spacer with an MDI mainly:",["Increases oropharyngeal deposition","Reduces oropharyngeal deposition","Is designed for use with DPIs","Increases the dose needed per puff"],1,"Less thrush and better lung delivery, especially in children."),
 ("Dry powder inhalers require:",["A slow, gentle inhalation","A fast, forceful inhalation","A spacer attached to the mouthpiece","Pressing the canister while inhaling"],1,"Breath-actuated; poor in patients with low inspiratory flow."),
 ('A 67-year-old woman with very severe COPD (FEV₁ 30%) and low peak inspiratory flow remains symptomatic on a dry powder inhaler despite a correct technique demonstration. She cannot inhale forcefully. Best next step:', ['Switch to an MDI with a spacer', 'Increase the DPI dose to twice daily', 'Add an oral short-acting β₂ agonist', 'Teach slower, gentler DPI inhalation'], 0, 'DPIs need fast, forceful inspiratory flow to disperse the powder; an MDI with spacer (or nebulizer) suits low-flow patients.', 2)],
"cromolyn":[("Cromolyn prevents asthma by:",["Direct β₂-mediated bronchodilation","Stabilizing mast cell membranes","Blocking CysLT1 (LTD₄) receptors","Binding and removing free IgE"],1,"Prevents mast cell degranulation; prophylactic only, not useful in an acute attack."),
 ("Cromolyn's main limitation:",["Severe systemic toxicity","No role in acute bronchospasm","Causes marked hyperkalemia","Frequent cardiac arrhythmias"],1,"Very safe but must be used before exposure."),
 ('A 10-year-old boy develops cough and wheeze 10 minutes into soccer practice. He is prescribed an inhaled agent to use 15 minutes before exercise; it has no bronchodilator effect and does not help once symptoms start. This agent works because it:', ['Stabilizes mast cells, preventing degranulation', 'Activates β₂ receptors to raise airway cAMP', 'Antagonizes CysLT1 receptors on airway muscle', 'Binds free IgE, preventing FcεRI binding'], 0, 'Cromolyn is a prophylactic mast cell stabilizer; β₂ agonists would bronchodilate and relieve active symptoms.', 2)],
"cftr":[("Ivacaftor works as a CFTR:",["Corrector (improves protein folding)","Potentiator (↑ open probability)","Gene therapy (adds normal CFTR)","Mucolytic (cleaves mucus DNA)"],1,"Ivacaftor increases channel open probability in gating mutations (G551D); lumacaftor/tezacaftor/elexacaftor are correctors."),
 ("Elexacaftor–tezacaftor–ivacaftor helps patients with at least one copy of:",["ΔF508 (F508del)","G551D only","Nonsense mutations only","Any CFTR deletion"],0,"Triple therapy targets the most common folding mutation."),
 ('A 14-year-old boy with cystic fibrosis has sweat chloride 98 mmol/L. Genotyping shows G551D on both alleles; the CFTR protein reaches the cell surface but rarely opens. Which drug best targets his defect?', ['Ivacaftor (CFTR potentiator)', 'Lumacaftor (CFTR corrector)', 'Dornase alfa (cleaves DNA)', 'Hypertonic saline (hydrates)'], 0, 'G551D is a gating mutation, so a potentiator that increases open probability is effective; correctors help folding defects such as F508del.', 3)],
"chronicasthma":[("Preferred step-1/2 approach in adolescents and adults (GINA):",["As-needed albuterol (SABA) alone","As-needed low-dose ICS–formoterol","Daily salmeterol (LABA) alone","Daily low-dose oral prednisone"],1,"SABA-only treatment is no longer recommended."),
 ("Before stepping up therapy, first check:",["Inhaler technique and adherence","Serum total IgE level","High-resolution CT of the chest","Serum theophylline concentration"],0,"Poor technique and adherence are the commonest reasons for poor control."),
 ('A 28-year-old woman with asthma uses albuterol about 4 days a week and wakes with cough once a week. She takes no controller; spirometry shows FEV₁ 85% predicted with 15% reversibility. Best next step in management:', ['Start low-dose ICS–formoterol', 'Add salmeterol monotherapy', 'Start oral prednisone daily', 'Add oral theophylline daily'], 0, 'Persistent symptoms need an ICS-containing controller (low-dose ICS–formoterol per GINA); LABA without ICS increases asthma deaths.', 2)],
"acuteasthma":[("A sign of impending respiratory failure in acute asthma:",["Loud, diffuse expiratory wheeze","Rising PaCO₂ and a silent chest","Tachypnea with low PaCO₂","Persistent dry cough at night"],1,"A normal or rising CO₂ in a tachypneic patient suggests fatigue."),
 ("IV magnesium sulfate may be used in:",["Mild intermittent asthma at home","Severe, refractory asthma exacerbations","Stable COPD maintenance therapy","Seasonal allergic rhinitis flares"],1,"Given when severe exacerbations do not respond to initial therapy; Mg²⁺ relaxes bronchial smooth muscle."),
 ('A 22-year-old man in the ED with an asthma attack has received 1 hour of nebulized albuterol plus ipratropium and IV methylprednisolone. He is alert but still speaks in single words; peak flow is 30% of predicted, PaCO₂ 38 mm Hg. Best next therapy:', ['Intravenous magnesium sulfate', 'Inhaled salmeterol added on', 'Oral montelukast loading', 'Intravenous aminophylline'], 0, 'IV magnesium is the recommended add-on for severe exacerbations refractory to initial therapy; aminophylline adds toxicity with little benefit.', 2)],
"stablecopd":[("Initial maintenance bronchodilator for most symptomatic COPD:",["ICS monotherapy","LAMA or LAMA+LABA","Oral theophylline","Daily oral steroids"],1,"Long-acting bronchodilators are the mainstay; ICS added for frequent exacerbations with eosinophilia."),
 ("Which intervention improves survival in COPD with chronic hypoxemia?",["Long-term oxygen therapy","Inhaled corticosteroids","Daily oral mucolytics","Codeine antitussives"],0,"Smoking cessation and LTOT (PaO₂ ≤ 55 mm Hg) reduce mortality."),
 ('A 68-year-old ex-smoker with COPD on LAMA + LABA has had 2 exacerbations needing oral prednisone in the past year. Blood eosinophil count is 400/µL. Best next step in maintenance therapy:', ['Add an inhaled corticosteroid', 'Add oral theophylline', 'Switch to a LAMA alone', 'Add daily oral prednisone'], 0, 'Frequent exacerbations with eosinophils ≥ 300/µL favor escalation to LAMA + LABA + ICS; chronic oral steroids are not recommended.', 2)],
"copdexac":[("Antibiotics in COPD exacerbation are indicated with:",["Any new cough without other symptoms","Purulent sputum plus ↑dyspnea or volume","Fever alone without sputum change","Clear sputum with stable dyspnea"],1,"Anthonisen criteria; common organisms: H. influenzae, S. pneumoniae, M. catarrhalis."),
 ("Oxygen target in COPD exacerbation:",["SpO₂ 98–100%","SpO₂ 88–92%","SpO₂ 80%","No oxygen"],1,"Over-oxygenation worsens hypercapnia."),
 ('A 70-year-old man with COPD presents with worse dyspnea and purulent sputum; SpO₂ 82%. He is given high-flow oxygen to SpO₂ 99% and becomes drowsy, with PaCO₂ rising from 52 to 75 mm Hg. The main mechanism is:', ['↑V/Q mismatch from loss of hypoxic vasoconstriction', 'Increased CO₂ production from fever and sepsis', 'Metabolic alkalosis from systemic corticosteroids', 'Direct depression of the medullary chemoreceptors'], 0, 'Excess O₂ releases hypoxic pulmonary vasoconstriction (worsening V/Q) and adds a Haldane effect; loss of hypoxic drive plays only a minor role. Target SpO₂ 88–92%.', 3)],
}
